zebkit: user worlds and node resolution in a world for the inspector protocol

Chrome's protocol has isolated worlds: a client makes a second JavaScript
world in a frame, with the same document but its own global object, runs
its helper scripts there out of the page's reach, and carries nodes between
that world and the page's. Client libraries keep their own machinery in one.
The CDP endpoint (src/cdp) translates to WebKit's inspector protocol, which
can evaluate in any world that exists but can neither make one nor hand a
node to one. This adds the two things that are missing:

- Page.setBootstrapScript takes an optional worldName. With it, the page
  agent creates a user world of that name in every frame, now and in every
  frame and document to come, and runs the script in each new global object
  of that world. Without it the command is what it was.
- DOM.resolveNode takes an optional executionContextId and then returns the
  node's wrapper in that context, which has to be one of the node's own
  document.

Both parameters are optional and nothing changes for a client that does not
send them. The same two additions are in the WebKit that Playwright ships.

